at when you learn  for the gmat the pareto principle or the  80 20 rule really holds true after a  couple of weeks of preparation you very  quickly reach a certain plateau and if  you reach that and then you want to  further improve the time that you need  to improve further will just go up  exponentially and at some point you just  need to ask yourself whether the time  invest is worth it or not for me  probably that point came after two weeks  after two weeks i started to realize  that my progress really slowed down  significantly and my strategy was that  already had the gmat test date after  week three of my preparation and if i  would not have achieved the test result  that i wanted i would probably have  rescheduled to another test date in the  future because as you might know you are  able to directly cancel your gmat test  results in a few minutes after you  complete the test the next key piece of  preparation advice is that you should  really do mock tests and the one that i  like best that i felt like resembled  most the actual test in the end was the  manhattan mock test you can either buy  exit to it on their website actually if  you get some of their books and i will  recommend you later some of them the  test access is already included with  them there are others as well i just  felt that the manhattan prep exam really  closely resembles the real thing in the  end so how exactly did i do it so after  two or three days of studying so after i  got the basic concept of what the gmat  was all about i decided to take the very  first mock exam to set a baseline for  myself so to understand from where i'm  starting and i remember that after these  two or three days i scored slightly  above 600 so it was a 6 10 or 6 20 or  something like that and from then on one  time per week and sometimes even two  times per week i took a new version of  the mock exam to track my progress and  understand where i'm standing this  helped me to get a live feedback whether  i'm on the right path whether learning  the studying that i'm doing is really  helping me to advance my score and in  fact the last mock exam that i took  roughly half a week before i took the  actual exam i scored to 710 which was  absolutely in the range of the final  result than i got in the real test the  next preparation advice is that while i  was studying i studied on some actual  real gmat test prep paper so i'm not  sure if you know but on the actual gmat  exam the type of paper you can use to  make notes it's standardized and it's  not a normal piece of paper instead it's  foiled paper and you can buy a paper  that very closely resembles this foil  paper here i got a set that i bought  myself that i use and as you can see it  has a special glossy foiled surface so  of course it's not hugely different than  writing a normal paper but at least you  should be aware of that and i just  wanted that my preparation comes as  close as possible to the actual test  experience so i bought this prep set i  will leave your link below down in the  description and as you can see there's  still some notes from the time when i  really learned for the gmat myself so it  made me a bit sentimental when i  actually looked at this but whenever i  did mock exercises whenever i studied i  made a point of always trying to take  notes and to write on this actual gmat  paper and of course it's not only a  paper but it's also the pen you need a  special foil marker in order to write on  this paper you will given that at the  test center but i found it helpful to  have already written with it to have  already worked with it in advance so it  was no surprise to me at all and my last  little prep advice is that even though  it's hard work and it's very serious for  all of us to get this test right still  try to see the fun of it and the use of  it beyond the exam itself so i found  that for me just preparing and studying  for the gmat exam that really helped  improve my quantitative skills so you  will take something out of it that will  go beyond the exam date itself there's a  use beyond the actual gmat test the next  thing i want to talk about is the  economist gmat tutor this is a program  that i found insanely helpful and  valuable when studying for my gmat exam  at the core of it you have an online  program that teaches you all the  strategies all the tips you need to  improve for the gmat and it also  includes lots and lots of mock exercises  besides that also included are some  private one-on-one video chat tutor  sessions with some experienced gmat  instructors you can submit essays that  they will rate and one of the best  things i thought is that they have a  score improvement guarantee of 50 to 70  points depending on which program you  take and 50 points are just huge 50  points are an absolute make or break of  your gmat exam so if you already took  the exam and want to improve your score  they guarantee you that with your  program you can improve by this amount  of points and if you don't you get your  money back and if you're a first-time  exam taker they will do a base exam at  the beginning to get your baseline and  then we'll guarantee to you that they  will improve you at least by that number  of points and if you don't you get your  money back and i have to admit it's  quite pricey it's quite an expensive  program but this way i really  rationalized it for me that if i do not  get the result that i want i would just  get my money back and hey for me it  worked really well so if you want to  check this out as well i have a link to  this course below in the video  description and the best thing is that  if you sign up via the link you can get  a seven day free trial and in these  seven days you can check out all the  functions by yourself for free so you  can really get a good feel whether this  is something that works for you and that  adds value for you so let's do some  brief and candid reviews of the books  and other materials 